     1→\section{Proposed Method}
     2→\label{sec:proposed_method}
     3→
     4→\subsection{Overview}
     5→
     6→Our methodology addresses multi-modal multi-resolution data fusion through a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that extends multilevel fusion paradigms \cite{ma2024multilevel} to handle fundamental challenges in operational satellite data fusion. While recent work demonstrated effective multilevel CNN-Transformer fusion for urban scene classification with uniform-resolution inputs \cite{ma2024multilevel}, operational earth observation presents distinct challenges: (1) \textit{heterogeneous sensor resolutions} where training labels (60m GFSC), SAR inputs (Sentinel-1 GRDH: 10m), and optical inputs (Sentinel-2: 10-60m) exhibit inherent resolution mismatches; (2) \textit{continuous-valued regression targets} (fractional snow cover 0-1) rather than discrete semantic classes requiring specialized loss functions and evaluation metrics; (3) \textit{variable data quality} from atmospheric conditions and sensor artifacts demanding adaptive sample weighting; and (4) \textit{operational reliability requirements} necessitating calibrated uncertainty quantification for decision-making.
     7→
     8→CrossFuse addresses these challenges through three principal innovations: (1) multi-resolution processing enabling training on coarse-resolution labels while producing high-resolution predictions via learned aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) cross-modal attention fusion with learnable modality-specific weighting that adaptively balances intra-modal and inter-modal information flow, and (3) quality-aware learning leveraging auxiliary metadata through confidence-weighted losses and curriculum strategies. Figure~\ref{fig:overall_methodology} illustrates the complete processing pipeline.
     9→
    10→\begin{figure*}[t]
    11→    \centering
    12→    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/overall_methodology.png}
    13→    \caption{The model consists of dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR, ECA for Sentinel-2 optical) that extract multi-scale features through four stages (64, 128, 256, 512 channels). Shallow Feature Fusion (SFF) modules merge cross-modal information at each scale, with skip connections (orange dashed arrows) propagating multi-resolution features to the decoder. Three novel contributions are highlighted: (1) Multi-Resolution Processing enables training on coarse 60m labels while processing fine 10m inputs through learned adaptive aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) the Fusion Vision Transformer (FVit) employs learnable  adaptive fusion weights ($\lambda_{sa}$, $\lambda_{ca}$) in its Ada-MBA stage to dynamically balance  self-attention and cross-attention based on data quality, and (3) Quality-Aware Training incorporates cloud   masks and quality flags through confidence-weighted loss with curriculum learning. The decoder produces  dual outputs: fractional snow cover prediction $\hat{y}$ and uncertainty estimate $\sigma^2$.}
    14→    \label{fig:overall_methodology}
    15→\end{figure*}
    16→
    17→\textbf{Problem Formulation.} Given paired multi-modal satellite observations $\mathbf{X}_{M1} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_1 \times H_1 \times W_1}$ and $\mathbf{X}_{M2} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_2 \times H_2 \times W_2}$ from heterogeneous sensors with potentially different resolutions $(H_1, W_1) \neq (H_2, W_2)$ and channels $C_1 \neq C_2$, our objective is to learn a fusion function $f_\theta: (\mathbf{X}_{M1}, \mathbf{X}_{M2}) \rightarrow \mathbf{Y}$ that produces predictions $\mathbf{Y} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H_f \times W_f}$ at target resolution $(H_f, W_f)$ with calibrated uncertainty estimates $\mathbf{\sigma}^2 \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H_f \times W_f}$. The key challenge is that training labels $\mathbf{Y}_{gt}$ are available only at coarse resolution $(H_c, W_c)$ where $H_c < H_f$ and $W_c < W_f$, while we desire high-resolution predictions that leverage fine-scale input information.
    18→
    19→\subsection{Multi-Resolution Processing Framework}
    20→
    21→The multi-resolution framework operates at three resolution stages: high-resolution input fusion, adaptive aggregation to match coarse-resolution training labels, and guided disaggregation to produce enhanced high-resolution predictions (Figure~\ref{fig:multi_resolution}).
    22→
    23→\begin{figure}[t]
    24→    \centering
    25→    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/multiresoulution_processing_framwork.png}
    26→    \caption{During training (\textcolor{orange}{orange}), Sentinel-1 GRDH/Sentinel-2 features at native resolution (10m, $[B,C,H,W]$) are adaptively aggregated to 60m ($[B,C,16,16]$) using max pooling for high-confidence snow pixels ($P>\tau$, $\tau$=0.7) and mean pooling otherwise, supervised by GFSC reference labels (60m, \textcolor{red}{red dashed}). During inference (\textcolor{purple}{purple}), the model produces 60m predictions that are disaggregated to 10m resolution ($[B,1,20,20]$) through guided filtering ($\hat{X} = a \cdot G(y) + b$) using high-resolution Sentinel-2 guidance for edge preservation and detail enhancement. Dual outputs include fractional snow cover prediction $\hat{y} \in [0,1]$ and uncertainty estimate $\sigma^2 \in \mathbb{R}^+$. Multi-scale loss $L = \sum \lambda_k L_k$ enforces consistency across resolutions.}
    27→    \label{fig:multi_resolution}
    28→\end{figure}
    29→
    30→\textbf{Resolution Aggregation.} During training, high-resolution features $\mathbf{F}_{hr} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C \times H_f \times W_f}$ are aggregated to match coarse-resolution targets through adaptive pooling:
    31→
    32→\begin{equation}
    33→\mathbf{F}_{cr} = \mathcal{A}(\mathbf{F}_{hr}) =
    34→\begin{cases}
    35→\max_{W_k} \mathbf{F}_{hr} & \text{if } P_{target}(W_k) > \tau \\
    36→\text{avg}_{W_k} \mathbf{F}_{hr} & \text{otherwise}
    37→\end{cases}
    38→\label{eq:adaptive_pooling}
    39→\end{equation}
    40→
    41→where $W_k$ is the aggregation window of size $k \times k$ with $k = H_f/H_c$, and $P_{target}$ is the learned target probability within the window computed through a lightweight detection network:
    42→
    43→\begin{equation}
    44→P_{target}(W_k) = \frac{1}{|W_k|} \sum_{(i,j) \in W_k} \sigma(f_{detect}(\mathbf{F}_{hr}(i,j)))
    45→\label{eq:target_probability}
    46→\end{equation}
    47→
    48→This adaptive strategy employs max pooling for target-dominated regions (preserving peak signals) and average pooling elsewhere (maintaining spatial consistency), with threshold $\tau = 0.7$.
    49→
    50→\textbf{Guided Disaggregation.} During inference, coarse-resolution predictions are disaggregated to high resolution through guided filtering \cite{he2012guided}:
    51→
    52→\begin{equation}
    53→\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{hr} = a \cdot \mathbf{G}_{hr} + b
    54→\label{eq:guided_filter}
    55→\end{equation}
    56→
    57→where $\mathbf{G}_{hr}$ is the high-resolution guidance image derived from input features, and coefficients $(a, b)$ are computed through local linear regression:
    58→
    59→\begin{equation}
    60→a = \frac{\text{Cov}(\mathbf{G}, \mathbf{Y})}{\text{Var}(\mathbf{G}) + \epsilon}, \quad b = \mu_{\mathbf{Y}} - a \cdot \mu_{\mathbf{G}}
    61→\label{eq:guided_coefficients}
    62→\end{equation}
    63→
    64→with $\mu$ denoting local means computed over sliding windows and $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ preventing division by zero. This preserves edge information from high-resolution inputs while maintaining consistency with coarse-resolution predictions.
    65→
    66→\textbf{Multi-Scale Loss.} Training incorporates multi-scale supervision:
    67→
    68→\begin{equation}
    69→\mathcal{L}_{ms} = \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} \lambda_k \cdot \mathcal{L}_{base}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_k, \mathbf{Y}_k)
    70→\label{eq:multiscale_loss}
    71→\end{equation}
    72→
    73→where $\lambda_k = 2^{-k}$ provides higher weight to finer scales, and $k=0$ corresponds to the target resolution. This encourages spatial coherence across the resolution hierarchy.
    74→
    75→\subsection{Hybrid CNN-Transformer Architecture}
    76→
    77→The architecture synergistically combines CNNs for local feature extraction with transformers for global context modeling, specifically designed for multi-modal fusion.
    78→
    79→\textbf{Dual-Path CNN Backbones.} Separate CNN pathways process heterogeneous modalities through structurally similar but parameter-independent networks (Figure~\ref{fig:overall_methodology}). Each pathway follows a ResNet-inspired architecture with four progressive stages:
    80→
    81→\begin{equation}
    82→\mathbf{F}_m^{(l)} = \mathcal{B}^{(l)}(\mathbf{F}_m^{(l-1)}), \quad m \in \{M1, M2\}, \; l \in \{1,2,3,4\}
    83→\label{eq:cnn_backbone}
    84→\end{equation}
    85→
    86→where $\mathcal{B}^{(l)}$ represents the $l$-th stage with channel dimensions $[64, 128, 256, 512]$ and spatial downsampling factors $[4, 8, 16, 32]$. Each block incorporates:
    87→
    88→\begin{equation}
    89→\mathcal{B}^{(l)}(\mathbf{F}) = \text{Attention}(\text{GELU}(\text{GroupNorm}(\text{Conv}(\mathbf{F}))))
    90→\label{eq:cnn_block}
    91→\end{equation}
    92→
    93→Group normalization (32 groups) handles varying batch sizes, GELU activation provides smooth gradients, and modality-specific attention mechanisms enhance feature quality. For the SAR modality (M1), we employ CBAM, which combines channel and spatial attention:
    94→
    95→
    96→\begin{equation}
    97→\begin{split}
    98→\mathbf{F}_{M1}^{att} = \mathbf{F}_{M1} \otimes \sigma(\text{MLP}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{F}_{M1}))) \\
    99→\otimes \sigma(\text{Conv}_{7\times7}([\text{GAP}(\mathbf{F}_{M1}); \text{GMP}(\mathbf{F}_{M1})]))
   100→\end{split}
   101→\label{eq:cbam}
   102→\end{equation}
   103→
   104→
   105→For optical modality (M2), Efficient Channel Attention emphasizes spectral relationships:
   106→
   107→\begin{equation}
   108→\mathbf{F}_{M2}^{att} = \mathbf{F}_{M2} \otimes \sigma(\text{Conv}_{1D}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{F}_{M2})))
   109→\label{eq:eca}
   110→\end{equation}
   111→
   112→% \begin{figure}[t]
   113→%     \centering
   114→%     \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cnn_backbone_arch.png}
   115→%     \caption{Dual-path CNN backbone architecture showing separate processing pathways for heterogeneous modalities with modality-specific attention mechanisms and progressive feature extraction through four hierarchical stages.}
   116→%     \label{fig:cnn_backbone}
   117→% \end{figure}
   118→
   119→\textbf{Cross-Attention Fusion Mechanism.} Multi-modal fusion employs bidirectional cross-attention to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_attention}). Our design builds upon multilevel fusion principles \cite{ma2024multilevel} while introducing \textit{learnable modality-specific fusion weights} that adaptively balance intra-modal self-attention with inter-modal cross-attention based on local scene characteristics and data quality---a critical extension for handling variable reliability across heterogeneous satellite sensors.
   120→
   121→For features $\mathbf{F}_{M1} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C \times H \times W}$ and $\mathbf{F}_{M2} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C \times H \times W}$, we compute:
   122→
   123→\begin{equation}
   124→\text{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)\mathbf{V}
   125→\label{eq:cross_attention}
   126→\end{equation}
   127→
   128→\begin{equation}
   129→\begin{aligned}
   130→\mathbf{F}_{M1 \rightarrow M2} &= \text{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{W}_Q^{M1}\mathbf{F}_{M1}, \mathbf{W}_K^{M2}\mathbf{F}_{M2}, \mathbf{W}_V^{M2}\mathbf{F}_{M2}) \\
   131→\mathbf{F}_{M2 \rightarrow M1} &= \text{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{W}_Q^{M2}\mathbf{F}_{M2}, \mathbf{W}_K^{M1}\mathbf{F}_{M1}, \mathbf{W}_V^{M1}\mathbf{F}_{M1})
   132→\end{aligned}
   133→\label{eq:bidirectional_fusion}
   134→\end{equation}
   135→
   136→Critically, we introduce \textit{learnable adaptive fusion weights} $\lambda_{sa}^{M1}, \lambda_{ca}^{M1}, \lambda_{sa}^{M2}, \lambda_{ca}^{M2}$ that balance self-attention (SA) and cross-attention (CA) contributions:
   137→
   138→\begin{equation}
   139→\begin{aligned}
   140→\mathbf{F}_{M1}^{fused} &= \lambda_{sa}^{M1} \cdot \text{SA}(\mathbf{F}_{M1}) + \lambda_{ca}^{M1} \cdot \mathbf{F}_{M2 \rightarrow M1} \\
   141→\mathbf{F}_{M2}^{fused} &= \lambda_{sa}^{M2} \cdot \text{SA}(\mathbf{F}_{M2}) + \lambda_{ca}^{M2} \cdot \mathbf{F}_{M1 \rightarrow M2}
   142→\end{aligned}
   143→\label{eq:adaptive_fusion_weights}
   144→\end{equation}
   145→
   146→where weights are learned during training to automatically determine optimal fusion strategies. For instance, when optical data is obscured by clouds, the network learns to increase $\lambda_{ca}^{M2}$ (relying more on SAR information) while decreasing $\lambda_{sa}^{M2}$ (down-weighting unreliable optical features). This adaptive mechanism addresses a fundamental limitation of fixed-weight fusion strategies \cite{ma2024multilevel} that cannot adjust to spatiotemporally varying data quality.
   147→
   148→The final fused representation combines both modalities:
   149→
   150→\begin{equation}
   151→\mathbf{F}_{fused} = \text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{W}_O[\mathbf{F}_{M1}^{fused} \oplus \mathbf{F}_{M2}^{fused}])
   152→\label{eq:fusion_output}
   153→\end{equation}
   154→
   155→where $\oplus$ denotes concatenation along the channel dimension. Multi-head attention (8 heads, $d_k=64$) enables capturing different aspects of cross-modal relationships simultaneously.
   156→
   157→\begin{figure*}[t]
   158→    \centering
   159→    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/cross_attention_fusion.png}
   160→    \caption{Cross-attention fusion mechanism showing bidirectional query-key-value transformations that explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies between heterogeneous modalities.}
   161→    \label{fig:cross_attention}
   162→\end{figure*}
   163→
   164→\textbf{Complexity Analysis.} Cross-attention computational complexity is $O(H W \cdot C \cdot d_k + (HW)^2 \cdot d_k)$ where the first term represents Q/K/V projections and the second term attention computation. For typical feature maps ($H=W=32$, $C=512$, $d_k=64$), this requires $\sim$1.1 GFLOPs per forward pass.
   165→
   166→\textbf{Spatial Transformer.} The transformer component captures long-range dependencies through spatial self-attention with learnable relative position encoding:
   167→
   168→\begin{equation}
   169→\text{SpatialAttn}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V})_{i,j} = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{q}_i \mathbf{k}_j^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{B}_{rel}(i,j)\right)\mathbf{v}_j
   170→\label{eq:spatial_attention}
   171→\end{equation}
   172→
   173→where $\mathbf{B}_{rel}(i,j) = \mathbf{W}_B[\text{pos}(i) - \text{pos}(j)]$ represents learnable relative position bias. The transformer employs 6 sequential blocks with 8 attention heads and 512 embedding dimensions, operating on compact feature representations ($H/32 \times W/32$) for computational efficiency:
   174→
   175→\begin{equation}
   176→\begin{aligned}
   177→\mathbf{Z}^{(0)} &= \text{PatchEmbed}(\mathbf{F}_{fused}) \\
   178→\mathbf{Z}^{(l)} &= \text{TransformerBlock}^{(l)}(\mathbf{Z}^{(l-1)}), \quad l \in \{1,\ldots,6\}
   179→\end{aligned}
   180→\label{eq:transformer_stack}
   181→\end{equation}
   182→
   183→Each transformer block consists of multi-head self-attention followed by feed-forward network with residual connections:
   184→
   185→\begin{equation}
   186→\begin{aligned}
   187→\mathbf{Z}' &= \mathbf{Z} + \text{MultiHeadAttn}(\text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{Z})) \\
   188→\mathbf{Z}_{out} &= \mathbf{Z}' + \text{FFN}(\text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{Z}'))
   189→\end{aligned}
   190→\label{eq:transformer_block}
   191→\end{equation}
   192→
   193→\textbf{Feature Pyramid Network.} Multi-scale processing through FPN addresses scale variability through attention-weighted fusion:
   194→
   195→\begin{equation}
   196→\mathbf{F}_{pyr}^{(l)} = \mathbf{A}^{(l)} \odot \mathbf{F}_{lat}^{(l)} + (1 - \mathbf{A}^{(l)}) \odot \text{Upsample}(\mathbf{F}_{pyr}^{(l+1)})
   197→\label{eq:fpn}
   198→\end{equation}
   199→
   200→where $\mathbf{A}^{(l)} = \sigma(\text{Conv}_{1\times1}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{F}_{lat}^{(l)})))$ represents learned attention weights determining optimal scale. The FPN processes features at four pyramid levels (P2-P5) with 256-channel feature maps.
   201→
   202→\subsection{Quality-Aware Learning Framework}
   203→
   204→\textbf{Confidence-Weighted Training.} Quality information from multiple sources (cloud masks, atmospheric parameters, sensor quality flags) is integrated through multiplicative loss weighting:
   205→
   206→\begin{equation}
   207→\mathcal{L}_{quality} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i^{conf} \cdot w_i^{qual} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{base}(\mathbf{y}_i, \hat{\mathbf{y}}_i)
   208→\label{eq:quality_loss}
   209→\end{equation}
   210→
   211→where $w_i^{conf} = \sigma(c_i)^\alpha$ represents confidence weight with strength parameter $\alpha=2$, and $w_i^{qual} = \prod_{j=1}^{K} q_{i,j}^{\beta_j}$ denotes quality mask weight from $K$ auxiliary indicators with $\beta_j \in [0.5, 1.5]$.
   212→
   213→\textbf{Curriculum Learning.} Training progresses from high-confidence to challenging samples via exponential decay:
   214→
   215→\begin{equation}
   216→\tau(e) = \max(\tau_{init} \cdot \exp(-\lambda \cdot e/E), \tau_{final})
   217→\label{eq:curriculum}
   218→\end{equation}
   219→
   220→with $\tau_{init}=0.9$, $\tau_{final}=0.3$, $\lambda=3$, ensuring stable early learning while gradually exposing the model to degraded conditions.
   221→
   222→\textbf{Uncertainty Quantification.} Pixel-wise uncertainty estimates are provided through a dedicated head with softplus activation:
   223→
   224→\begin{equation}
   225→\sigma^2 = \log(1 + \exp(\mathbf{z}))
   226→\label{eq:uncertainty}
   227→\end{equation}
   228→
   229→regularized through negative log-likelihood encouraging higher uncertainty for larger errors:
   230→
   231→\begin{equation}
   232→\mathcal{L}_{unc} = \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^2) + \frac{(\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}})^2}{2\sigma^2}
   233→\label{eq:nll_loss}
   234→\end{equation}
   235→
   236→\textbf{Complete Loss Function.} The total training objective integrates multiple components:
   237→
   238→\begin{equation}
   239→\mathcal{L}_{total} = \alpha \mathcal{L}_{regression} + \beta \mathcal{L}_{perceptual} + \gamma \mathcal{L}_{consistency} + \delta \mathcal{L}_{unc}
   240→\label{eq:total_loss}
   241→\end{equation}
   242→
   243→Primary regression uses smooth L1: $\mathcal{L}_{regression} = \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Omega} w_{i,j} \cdot \text{SmoothL1}(\mathbf{y}_{i,j}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}_{i,j})$. Perceptual consistency employs VGG-based features: $\mathcal{L}_{perceptual} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \lambda_l \|\Phi_l(\mathbf{y}) - \Phi_l(\hat{\mathbf{y}})\|_2^2$. Spatial consistency maintains local coherence: $\mathcal{L}_{consistency} = \|\nabla \mathbf{y} - \nabla \hat{\mathbf{y}}\|_1$. Loss weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta)$ are learned through gradient-based optimization.
   244→
   245→\subsection{Training and Implementation}
   246→
   247→\textbf{Training Protocol.} End-to-end training uses AdamW optimizer \cite{loshchilov2017decoupled} with weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$ and effective batch size 64 via gradient accumulation. Learning rate follows cosine annealing with warm restarts: $\eta_t = \eta_{min} + \frac{1}{2}(\eta_{max} - \eta_{min})(1 + \cos(\frac{T_{cur}}{T_{max}}\pi))$ where $\eta_{max}=10^{-4}$, $\eta_{min}=10^{-6}$, initializing at $\eta_{max}$ over 100 epochs. Mixed-precision training (FP16) with dynamic loss scaling reduces memory by $\sim$40\%. Regularization includes dropout (0.1), stochastic depth (0.1), and label smoothing (0.1). Data augmentation employs random cropping, flipping, rotation ($\pm 15°$), and Gaussian noise ($\sigma=0.01$).
   248→
   249→% \textbf{Computational Efficiency.} The architecture processes patches with $\sim$45M parameters and $\sim$12 GFLOPs. Grouped normalization reduces parameters, hierarchical CNN structure minimizes complexity via progressive downsampling, and transformer operation on compact representations significantly reduces quadratic complexity. Gradient checkpointing for transformer layers trades computation for memory.
   250→
   251→% \textbf{Inference Strategy.} For large images, sliding window prediction with 64-pixel overlap is employed. Overlapping regions are blended using Gaussian weights eliminating boundary artifacts. Test-time augmentation with 8-fold geometric transformations (rotations, reflections) enhances reliability through ensemble averaging.
